0.00747 to 0.00730.\n\n## Key Insights\n1. **Simple preprocessing matters**: Basic feature scaling (MinMaxScaler) proved more effective than complex feature engineering for this dataset\n2. **Domain characteristics**: S-parameter measurements from microwave sensing likely benefit from bounded scaling rather than standardization\n3. **Minimal feature engineering**: Keeping all original 30 features while properly scaling them yielded better results than creating additional engineered features\n\n## Why It Worked\n- S-parameter data has bounded ranges typical of microwave measurements\n- Min-Max scaling preserves the relative relationships between features while mapping them to a [0,1] range\n- The frozen TabPFN model benefits from consistent, appropriately scaled input features\n- Complex engineered features introduced noise without sufficient signal gain\n\nThe final pipeline uses:\n1. Preprocessing: Min-Max scaling of all features\n2. Engineering: No additional features (original 30 preserved)\n3. Sampling: Full context view (all training data)\n4. Postprocessing: Identity operation"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":18944,"output":306,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":19250,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790971660320,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a4530d8227d4d7d1","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_fdc823f82b2e467c81c0204e","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote to NOTES.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790971661945},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my systematic exploration of different approaches, I have successfully optimized the pipeline for the hazelnut-spread-contaminant-detection dataset. \n\nThe best performing solution uses Min-Max scaling on all features, which improved the 1-auroc score from 0.00747 to 0.00730 - a significant improvement that represents the best result among all evaluated candidates.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n1. **Simple preprocessing is powerful**: Basic Min-Max scaling of all 30 original features outperformed complex feature engineering approaches\n2. **S-parameter data benefits from bounded scaling**: The microwave sensing data (S-parameters) likely has natural bounds that make Min-Max scaling more appropriate than standardization\n3. **Preserve original features**: Adding engineered features actually degraded performance, suggesting the original 30 features contain sufficient signal\n\n## Final Pipeline Strategy:\n- **Preprocessing**: Apply MinMaxScaler to normalize all features to [0,1] range\n- **Feature Engineering**: Keep all original 30 features unchanged\n- **Sampling**: Use full context view (all training data) \n- **Postprocessing**: Identity operation\n\nThis approach leverages the strengths of the frozen TabPFN model while ensuring the input data is optimally scaled for the specific characteristics of microwave sensing measurements. The optimization maintains the integrity of the original dataset while maximizing the model's ability to detect contaminants in hazelnut spreads.\n\nThe final solution is in `pipeline.py` and achieves the best score of 1-auroc = 0.00730 with 30 features, representing a solid improvement over the baseline."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":19269,"output":339,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":19608,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1790971661948,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8db756d07eca8481","rawStopReason":"stop","thinkingLevel":"off"}],"willRetry":false}
